slides.markdown:
Both of these slide a panel 320px to the right over 300ms. They are not the same animation.

```css
/* Janky: every frame re-enters at Layout */
.panel {
  position: absolute;
  left: -320px;
  transition: left 300ms ease-out;
}
.panel.open { left: 0; }

/* Smooth: every frame enters at Composite */
.panel {
  position: absolute;
  left: 0;
  transform: translateX(-320px);
  transition: transform 300ms ease-out;
}
.panel.open { transform: translateX(0); }
```

The `left` version asks the browser to recompute geometry on **every single frame** — around 18 frames for a 300ms transition, each one running layout, paint, and composite on the main thread. If any of them exceeds the ~10ms budget, the frame is dropped and the motion visibly stutters.

The `transform` version paints the panel once, then hands the compositor a new translation matrix each frame. Layout and paint run zero times during the animation.

The same rule applies to size: animate `transform: scale()` instead of `width`/`height`, and fade with `opacity` rather than toggling `visibility` or `background-color` alpha.
